## The three parcels

The geography is the whole point of this address and it took the corpus

three separate passes to assemble, because each source holds one third of it.

**Fran's house** sits on the fairway. **Annie's grandparents — confirmed as

Jim and Sugie — held the directly adjacent house** for the same fifty-odd

years, the two families "super tight for fucking ever"; Dan's mother dated

Annie's father in high school. Two generations of entanglement were built

across that property line before Dan and Annie met. And per Dan's own

statement in a February 2026 session, **[[wiki/places/155-virginia-ave|155

Virginia Avenue]] is on the lot adjacent to this one** — the house

[[wiki/people/suzanne-frank|Suz]] owned, that Dan lived in from January 2015

to February 2019, and that the novelist [[wiki/people/jacob-bacharach]] used

as a fictional setting without knowing any of the rest of it.

So the caregiving job had no commute. Dan and Annie were living one lot away

from the woman they were being paid to keep at home, next door to Annie's

own grandparents, on ground the two families had shared since before either

of them was born. Dan gave the arrangement in his own words to

[[wiki/people/christo-coan|Christo Coan]] in October 2017 — on the golf

course, next door to both sets of grandparents, *"so noise isn't a

problem"* — which is the only first-person confirmation of the layout from

outside the family record.

The last piece is the smallest and the strangest. When Dan needed to explain

where the house was to somebody who did not know it, he did not give

cross-streets. He wrote **"we are the flag on 5 fairway"**, corrected

himself to *"er…beside 5 fairway"* — and the fifth fairway is the identical

landmark Bacharach used to locate the house next door in a novel. Two

different men, a generation apart and with no knowledge of each other's

purpose, fixed these two lots in writing by the same hole.

## The job

Fran's standing directive was categorical and she gave it to Dan and Suz

jointly: **stay in this house as long as humanly possible** — keep me here

no matter what, do everything you can to not let me be taken out of it. The

caregiving arrangement was built around that sentence. It was informal

across the mid-2010s and became, from roughly October 2017, paid full-time

work at $15/hour that ran the six months to her death, authorised through

the trustee who held her money.

The house is why the directive existed. Her position on the fairway was the

physical seat of decades of standing at the club — the 1969 hole-in-one, the

Women's Crown — and the record frames the two-week nursing-home collapse

that followed her removal as the confirmation that the house had been what

was keeping her alive. She did not ask to be kept comfortable. She asked to

be kept *here*.

## The night it ended, and what the house was being used for

The occupancy ended inside a single night with the house full, and the

corpus now dates the logistics of that night to three weeks before it.

On **February 16, 2018** — the day the [[wiki/people/danny-matthews|Danny

Matthews]] thread opens and the non-monogamy arrangement is negotiated —

Dan writes *"we have a great place for hangs too."* Danny asks where. Dan

sends a location card and then the address: **"117 belmont circle."** Danny

answers *"I'm pretty sure I hit a ball into your yard before,"* and the

conversation moves to golf balls and houses. The venue for the arrangement's

opening night was selected, named and pitched three weeks in advance, and

it was the house Dan was being paid to guard.

On the night of **March 7–8** the encounter went ahead. Fran's first fall

happened while Danny was still en route, and Dan — invoking her own

directive against calling an ambulance — put her back to bed. The second and

worse fall came in the minutes after the front door closed behind Danny. She

left on a stretcher, went to a rehabilitation nursing home, asked *when am I

leaving?* without stopping, and was dead four weeks later, on **April 4,

2018**. The full account of the night is at

[[wiki/timeline/events/the-fall-of-fran]]; the four weeks after it are at

[[wiki/timeline/events/fran-death-vigil]].

## The eight months the house belonged to Dan

This is the part of the property's record that no previous pass had, and it

changes what the house *is* in 2018. Across five documented occasions

between February and September 2018 — **four of them after Fran was dead** —

Dan gives out 117 Belmont Circle as the place to come and meet him:

| Date | Recipient | What the address was for |

|---|---|---|

| 2018-02-16 | [[wiki/people/danny-matthews|Danny Matthews]] | the arrangement's venue, pitched as "a great place for hangs" |

| 2018-04-12 | [[wiki/people/jim-vrabel|Jim Vrabel]] | a 1:52 AM invitation, eight days after the death |

| 2018-04-27 | a Nemacolin caddie coworker | somewhere to sleep instead of paying for a hotel |

| 2018-05-29 | "Big Lou," another caddie | staging point before a round |

| 2018-09-05 | [[wiki/people/bill|Bill]] | a pharmaceutical handoff |

The April 12 message is the one that says the most, because Dan is not

giving directions in it — he is describing where he lives: *"we are still at

my grandmas. my mom fell asleep… you're still welcome to stop by though."*

Suz asleep in the house, Dan awake in it at two in the morning, eight days

after the funeral, texting a visitor the address.

Two things follow. The first is that the house did not sit empty between the

death and the listing; it became the operating address of the

[[wiki/work/nemacolin-caddying|caddie yard's]] social layer and, by

September, of a supply thread. The second is that this sits directly against

the **eviction notice served at [[wiki/places/155-virginia-ave|155 Virginia

Avenue]] on March 29, 2018**, mid-vigil, demanding the keys. The residence

timeline has Dan at 155 Virginia until February 2019 and has always treated

that notice's outcome as unrecorded. The Belmont Circle usage does not

resolve it, but it supplies the missing option: through the spring and

summer of 2018 he had somewhere else to be, one lot away, and he was using

it.

